        <a href="#section-programmatic-api-common" id="section-programmatic-api-common" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Build a Mailer in Java</h2>

  <section>
    <div>
      <p>
Create a <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory and ask it for fresh builders for each Email or Mailer.
The builders use the factory's immutable configuration snapshot. Mailers from that factory also share
<a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-sending-limits">sending-limit history</a>; a separate factory has its own allowance.
The public builder interfaces provide the complete option reference.
</p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p>
        Without a configured transport strategy, the Mailer uses <code class="inline language-java">SMTP</code> with opportunistic TLS,
        even when you supply a username and password. It tries STARTTLS when advertised and may continue in plaintext when it is absent;
        a failed TLS upgrade stops the connection. Choose <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code> or
        <code class="inline language-java">SMTPS</code> if encryption is required. See the
        <a href="security.html#section-authentication-methods">transport strategy comparison</a> for the differences.
        <a href="security.html#section-authentication-methods">Mandatory STARTTLS settings</a> cannot be disabled through extra properties.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer(System.getenv("SMTP_HOST"), 587,
        System.getenv("SMTP_USER"), System.getenv("SMTP_PASSWORD"))
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
      <p>Use your server's port and transport strategy. In an application, reuse the Mailer and close it at shutdown.
        Configure settings through <a href="#section-config-properties">properties</a> or <a href="/spring.html">Spring</a> when that suits your application.
        For a proxy or a supplied Session, see the alternatives below.</p>
      <h3>Other connection setups</h3>
<pre><code class="small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

// start with a fresh builder
MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; mailerBuilder = mail.mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServer("smtp.host.com", 25, "username", "password");
// or
mailerBuilder = mail.mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServerHost("smtp.host.com")
  .withSMTPServerPort(25)
  .withSMTPServerUsername("username")
  .withSMTPServerPassword("password");
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">
// omit credentials for an anonymous server only if the factory has none configured
// a shorter overload preserves any credentials already on the builder
mail.mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.host.com", 25);
// or
mail.mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServerHost("smtp.host.com")
  .withSMTPServerPort(25);

// clear only these credentials without changing the host or port
mailerBuilder.clearSMTPServerUsername().clearSMTPServerPassword();

// require STARTTLS, also when authenticating with username/password
mailerBuilder.withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS);

// separate choice: explicitly skip STARTTLS for a plain SMTP connection
mailerBuilder.withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP)
  .withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
      .withOpportunisticTLS(false)
      .build());

// anonymous SOCKS proxy
mailerBuilder.withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
    .withHost("proxy.host.com")
    .withPort(1080)
    .build());

// or authenticated proxy
mailerBuilder
  .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
      .withHost("proxy.host.com")
      .withPort(1080)
      .withUsername("proxy username")
      .withPassword("proxy password")
      .build());
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">
// anonymous smtp + anonymous proxy + default SMTP protocol strategy
mailerBuilder
        .withSMTPServerHost("smtp.host.com").withSMTPServerPort(25)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("proxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .build());
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">
// configure everything!
mail.mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.host.com", 587, "user@host.com", "password")
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("socksproxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .withUsername("proxy user")
            .withPassword("proxy password")
            .build())
        .buildMailer()
        .sync().sendMail(email);
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">
// preconfigured Session?
mail.mailerBuilder(session);

// add an anonymous proxy to a Session without SOCKS routing
mail.mailerBuilder(session)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("socksproxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .build());

// add an authenticated proxy to a Session without SOCKS routing
mail.mailerBuilder(session)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("socksproxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .withUsername("proxy user")
            .withPassword("proxy password")
            .build());
</code></pre>
      <p>
Reuse a Mailer for concurrent sends and close it at application shutdown. Each Mailer needs its own Session and
<code>Properties</code>: neither can be used by another Mailer until shutdown succeeds. To create independent Sessions,
copy the original properties and use <code>Session.getInstance(...)</code>, rather than <code>Session.getDefaultInstance(...)</code>.
</p>
      <p>A supplied Session keeps its existing connection settings and its omitted settings. Ordinary factory connection defaults do not fill its gaps. Read <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#supplied-sessions">the supplied-Session rules</a> before customizing it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


<a href="#section-reusable-mailer" id="section-reusable-mailer" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 core=true}}Configure once, reuse many times{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
A Mailer is thread-safe. Configure it once and reuse it for the messages that share those settings.
</p>
    </div>

    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code>Mailer inhouseMailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("server", 25, "username", "password")
    .buildMailer();

inhouseMailer.sync().sendMail(email);
inhouseMailer.sync().sendMail(anotherEmail);
</code></pre>
      Or as preconfigured Spring bean:
<pre><code>@Bean(destroyMethod = "close")
public Mailer inhouseMailer(SimpleJavaMail mail) {
    return mail.mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer(...)
        .buildMailer();
}
</code></pre>
      Or inject the auto-configured default after adding the <a href="/spring.html">Spring Boot starter</a>:
<pre><code>@Autowired Mailer mailer; // configured through Spring's normal property sources</code></pre>
      <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-installation">Plain Spring</a> uses the same bean through
      <code class="inline language-java">@Import(SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport.class)</code>.
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-programmatic-api-other" id="section-programmatic-api-other" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure advanced Mailer settings</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>Say the checkout worker already sends receipts. Now it needs a shorter connection timeout and more time to read the relay’s reply.
        Put related choices in a <code>ConnectionConfig</code> and pass it to the Mailer builder.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withConnectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
    .withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(45))
    .build());</code></pre>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Which group holds my setting?</summary>
<div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Mailer configuration groups"><table><thead><tr><th>Mailer method</th><th>Configuration value</th><th>Settings</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>withConnectionPoolConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionPoolConfig</code></td><td>Connection counts, claim timeout, expiration and clustering.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withAsyncExecutionConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>AsyncExecutionConfig</code></td><td>Worker count, keep-alive, waiting queue and admission, or a supplied executor.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withProxyConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>SocksProxyConfig</code></td><td>SOCKS endpoint, credentials and local bridge port.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withSendingLimitsConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>SendingLimitsConfig</code></td><td>Message/recipient rates and shared allowance.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withConnectionConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionConfig</code></td><td>Socket timeouts, local binding, EHLO hostname and legacy content compatibility.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withTlsConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>TlsConfig</code></td><td>Trust, hostname checks, SSL factories and optional STARTTLS.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withConnectionConflictPolicy(...)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionConflictPolicy</code></td><td>Advanced handling of conflicting connection declarations.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withValidationConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>ValidationConfig</code></td><td>Address policy, warning behavior and maximum prepared email size.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div><p>For custom sending and diagnostics, see <a href="#section-extension-points">extension points</a>.</p>
</details>
      <p>When you pass a configuration group to the builder, it replaces the previous group you supplied. Use
        <code>toBuilder()</code> to change just one member. See what the Mailer ended up using with
        <code>getOperationalConfig()</code>.</p>

      <p id="explicit-connection-clears">Clearing a connection setting can remove an imported value and is subject to conflict checks. <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#explicit-connection-clears">Check the clearing and restoration rules</a> when working with loaded properties or a supplied Session.</p>
      <p><code>ConnectionConfig.Builder.withSessionTimeout(...)</code> sets connect, read and write timeouts together.
        Use <code>withConnectTimeout(...)</code>, <code>withReadTimeout(...)</code> or <code>withWriteTimeout(...)</code> to adjust one operation.
        The last setter affecting an operation wins; see the <a href="#socket-timeouts">timeout examples and precedence</a>.</p>
      <p>Builder getters inspect supplied choices; the built Mailer reports the resolved settings. See <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-diagnostics">which inspection to use</a> when tracing a value.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="wide">
<h3 id="configuration-operations">Setting, clearing and restoring a choice</h3>
<p><code>withX(...)</code> supplies a choice; <code>clearX()</code> removes it at the documented scope. <code>resetX()</code> chooses the library fallback, and <code>useConfiguredX()</code> restores captured configuration. For a small group edit, keep the existing value and change only the desired member: <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-operations">Read the detailed explanation</a>.</p>
</div>
<div class="wide"><pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()
    .withMaxSize(12)
    .build());</code></pre></div>

    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="small">// report missing fields, invalid addresses and CRLF suspicions as warnings,
// then continue with SMTP handling
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder()
    .withClientValidationWarningsOnly(true)
    .build());
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">// apply your own local email-size limit, independently of the SMTP server
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder()
    .withMaximumEmailSize(10_000_000)
    .build());</code></pre>
      <p>Managed Angus sends also check the prepared content against the connected server's advertised SIZE limit automatically.
        There is no additional property or builder setting. This connected check is separate from the local limit above and offline rehearsal;
        see <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-message-size">message size diagnostics</a>.</p>
<pre><code class="small">// make the underlying Jakarta Mail Session produce debug output
mailerBuilder
    .withDebugConfig(SessionDebugConfig.builder()
        .withEnabled(true)
        .withOutput(SessionDebugOutput.SLF4J) // STDOUT, STDERR or SLF4J
        .build());
</code></pre>
      <p><code>SessionDebugConfig</code> keeps the enabled flag and output selection together. On its builder, <code>withOutput(...)</code>
        and <code>withPrinter(...)</code> select one destination. <code>useConfiguredOutput()</code> restores the factory's captured output
        selection, leaving a supplied Session's output unchanged when no output was configured. Borrowed streams are never closed.</p>
      <p><code>getEnabled()</code>, <code>getOutput()</code> and <code>getPrinter()</code> inspect supplied choices; null means no explicit choice.
        <code>resetEnabled()</code> chooses the built-in false default, while <code>useConfiguredEnabled()</code> restores inheritance.
        Replace the complete group with <code>withDebugConfig(...)</code>, edit existing choices through <code>getDebugConfig().toBuilder()</code>,
        or restore all inherited choices with <code>useConfiguredDebugConfig()</code>. Omitted fields inherit the factory snapshot.
        <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getDebugConfig()</code> exposes the resolved choices.</p>
<pre><code class="small">// skip actually sending email, just log it
mailerBuilder.withTransportModeLoggingOnly(true);
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">// custom SSL connection factory (note: breaks setups with authenticated proxy!)
mailerBuilder.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
    .withCustomSSLFactoryClass("org.mypackage.MySSLFactory")
    // or an already constructed factory; an instance takes precedence over the class
    .withCustomSSLFactoryInstance(mySSLFactoryInstance)
    .build());
</code></pre>
      <p>On <code>TlsConfig</code> and its builder, inspect the instance with <code>getCustomSSLFactoryInstance()</code>, or inspect the class name with
        <code>getCustomSSLFactoryClass()</code>. Use <code>clearCustomSSLFactoryInstance()</code> to return to the configured class, or
        <code>clearCustomSSLFactoryClass()</code> to remove that class choice. Clearing one leaves the other intact; configuration locks still apply.</p>
      <p>If your custom factory fails, the connection attempt fails too when Simple Java Mail creates the Angus Session. It does not quietly switch to
        the standard factory, which might not use your client certificate or custom certificate checks. To deliberately allow that fallback, use the
        existing advanced property:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback", "true");</code></pre>
      <p>The property-file and Spring key is <code>simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback</code>.
        For SMTPS, replace <code>mail.smtp</code> with <code>mail.smtps</code> in either form. Only enable this if the standard factory is acceptable.
        See <a href="security.html#section-custom-ssl-factory">custom SSL factories</a> for connection cleanup and module-access details.</p>
<pre><code class="small">
// change email validation strategy
ValidationConfig.Builder validation = ValidationConfig.builder()
    .withEmailValidator(
        JMail.strictValidator()
            .requireOnlyTopLevelDomains(TopLevelDomain.DOT_COM)
            .withRule(email -> email.localPart().startsWith("allowed")));
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.build());

// restore the default strict JMail address validator:
validation.resetEmailValidator();
// clear the JMail address policy; keep completeness, encoded-word and CRLF checks:
validation.clearEmailValidator();
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.build());
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">
// set custom properties
mailerBuilder.withProperties(new Properties());
mailerBuilder.withProperties(new HashMap());
mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.smtp.timeout", 10000);

// advanced: change a property on the Mailer's Session:
mailer.getSession().getProperties().setProperty("mail.smtp.timeout", "10000");
</code></pre>
      <p>The builder and <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig()</code> return separate property containers for inspection. Editing those returned
        containers does not reconfigure either object. Use builder methods before construction, or explicitly access the Session for an advanced
        runtime change. Object-valued provider settings, such as a socket factory, retain their supplied identity.</p>
      <p><code>mailerBuilder.withProperties(properties)</code> copies only entries stored directly in that Java <code>Properties</code> object,
        not values inherited from its defaults. Add any inherited value explicitly with <code>withProperty(...)</code> if the Session needs it.
        This differs from <code>ConfigLoader.builder().withProperties(...)</code>, which includes inherited string defaults when loading factory settings.</p>
      <h3 id="session-construction-properties">Use your own executor for mail events</h3>
      <p>Use <code>withProperties(Properties)</code> to supply an executor for Jakarta Mail events, or other object-valued settings such as
        socket factories. <code>withProperty(String, Object)</code> converts the value to text, so it cannot pass an executor instance.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Properties extra = new Properties();
extra.setProperty("mail.event.scope", "session");
extra.put("mail.event.executor", eventExecutor);

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 25)
    .withProperties(extra)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>Your application owns the executor; Simple Java Mail does not shut it down. Put it directly in the <code>Properties</code> object,
        as shown above. An executor inherited through <code>Properties</code> defaults is not used.</p>
      <p>If you supply your own Session and use <code>mail.event.scope=session</code>, set the executor before calling
        <code>Session.getInstance(...)</code>. Requesting a different executor on the Mailer builder is rejected by <code>buildMailer()</code>,
        even with <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>. Supplying the same executor instance is accepted. For Jakarta Mail's default
        <code>folder</code> scope, you can supply the executor on the Mailer builder. Scope names such as <code>session</code> are case-insensitive.</p>
      <p>When you supply a Session, that instance is used for sending, including its Authenticator and custom mail providers.
        See <a href="debugging.html#section-debug-logging">debug logging</a> to configure its diagnostic output.</p>
      <h3 id="configure-relay-tls">Verify the relay’s certificate</h3>
      <p>The defaults check the certificate chain and the relay hostname. If your company signs its own certificates, add its issuing CA
        to the JVM trust store. The same Mailer can then verify the private relay.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Inspect or change TLS trust choices</summary>
<p><code>TlsConfig.builder().trustingAllHosts(false).verifyingServerIdentity(true)</code> expresses the secure defaults.
        If a private CA cannot be added, <code>trustingSSLHosts("smtp.internal.example")</code> scopes a trust exception to that host.
        <code>trustingAllHosts(true)</code> bypasses issuer checks for every host. Keep hostname verification enabled for ordinary sends.</p>
        <p>Edit the current group through <code>mailerBuilder.getTlsConfig().toBuilder()</code> and pass its built value to <code>withTlsConfig(...)</code>.
        <code>clearTrustedSSLHosts().resetTrustingAllHosts()</code> clears trust exceptions. If you disabled hostname verification
        with <code>verifyingServerIdentity(false)</code>, call <code>resetVerifyingServerIdentity()</code> to restore the default.</p>
</details>
      <h3 id="configure-async-workers">Give background sends their own workers</h3>
      <p>For a backlog of receipts, choose how many workers may send at once and how many tasks can wait.</p>
<pre><code class="small">// explicit worker settings apply with or without the batch module
mailerBuilder.withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
    .withThreadPoolSize(3)
    // Duration.ZERO: core threads stay alive; positive values retire idle workers
    .withKeepAliveTime(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
    // optional bound on waiting async tasks, separate from worker count
    .withQueueCapacity(100)
    .rejectWhenFull()
    .build());</code></pre>
      <p>The default is one worker without the batch module and ten with it. To use your own pool, supply
        <code>AsyncExecutionConfig.builder().withExecutorService(executor).build()</code> instead. Its queue and tuning come from your executor.</p>
      <p>Java uses <code>Duration</code> for Session, executor and pool times. Use whole milliseconds in properties and CLI arguments.
        Fractional milliseconds and overflow are rejected.</p>
      <h3 id="socket-timeouts">Socket timeouts</h3>
      <p>When your relay accepts connections quickly but takes longer to confirm a message, give reads more time than connects.
        Start with a shared value and adjust the operations that need it.</p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Socket timeout example by operation">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it limits</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Connect</th><td>Opening the socket to the relay.</td><td>10 seconds</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Read</th><td>Waiting for data, such as an SMTP reply.</td><td>45 seconds</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Write</th><td>Writing data to the socket.</td><td>30 seconds</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
<pre><code class="language-java">ConnectionConfig connection = ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withSessionTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .withConnectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
    .withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(45))
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(connection);</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Setter order.</strong> The last setter for an operation wins. Calling <code>withSessionTimeout(...)</code> again
          sets all three. Use <code>withWriteTimeout(...)</code> to adjust writes alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Supplied Session.</strong> Existing timeouts and omissions stay in place. Select
          <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code> to replace a conflicting setting deliberately. An individual timeout lock governs
          one operation and a shared timeout lock governs all three. Conflicts with locks fail at construction.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Socket timeouts apply to individual operations. For an overall time limit that includes time in the queue,
        use a <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-send-deadlines">send deadline</a>.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Defaults, property precedence and CLI options</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Managed Sessions.</strong> With <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>, each operation uses an explicit
            typed choice in <code>ConnectionConfig</code>, then an extra Jakarta Mail property, then an operation-specific SJM
            property, then the shared timeout property, then the built-in 60-second fallback. For example,
            <code>mail.smtp.connectiontimeout=10000</code> and <code>mail.smtp.timeout=30000</code> give 10/30/60 seconds
            with otherwise default configuration. A typed 45-second read timeout changes only the read value.
            The default <code>REJECT_CONFLICTS</code> policy rejects differing explicit declarations for an operation.
            A built-in fallback by itself cannot conflict with a raw value.</li>
          <li><strong>Reset or restore.</strong> <code>resetConnectTimeout()</code>, <code>resetReadTimeout()</code>
            and <code>resetWriteTimeout()</code> select the built-in 60-second value for one operation.
            Their <code>useConfigured...</code> counterparts restore captured inheritance. <code>resetSessionTimeout()</code>
            and <code>useConfiguredSessionTimeout()</code> apply these choices to all three.</li>
          <li><strong>CLI.</strong> Supply numeric milliseconds in the same order. The CLI also accepts
            <code>--mailer:withWriteTimeout</code>, <code>--mailer:resetConnectTimeout</code>,
            <code>--mailer:resetReadTimeout</code> and <code>--mailer:resetWriteTimeout</code>.</li>
        </ul>
<pre><code class="language-text small">--mailer:withSessionTimeout 30000 --mailer:withConnectTimeout 10000 --mailer:withReadTimeout 45000</code></pre>
        <p><code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getVerifyingServerIdentity()</code> returns a nullable <code>Boolean</code>.
          An omitted or unrecognized value on a supplied Session leaves its effective identity policy unspecified in this report.
          To request verification explicitly, use <code>TlsConfig.builder().verifyingServerIdentity(true).build()</code>
          within the limits of the connection conflict policy and locks. See
          <a href="security.html#section-verify-server-identity">server identity verification</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Write-timeout schedulers and connection pools</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>With write timeouts enabled, each Angus Session created by Simple Java Mail shares one monitoring thread across its
            connections. The monitor stops when the last connection closes and restarts when needed. Returning a connection to
            the pool leaves it open, so the monitor stays available.</li>
          <li>If you supply a scheduler via <code>mail.smtp.executor.writetimeout</code> or
            <code>mail.smtps.executor.writetimeout</code>, close it when your application is finished with it.
            Supplied Sessions and other mail providers keep their own timeout handling.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <h3 id="more-mailer-choices">When the relay or message needs another setting</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>For machines with multiple interfaces, choose the <a href="#section-local-bind-address">local source address</a>.
          To identify the service to the relay, set its <a href="#section-smtp-client-hostname">EHLO hostname</a>.</li>
        <li>To sign every receipt, configure <a href="security.html#section-sending-dkim">DKIM on an Email defaults template</a>.
          For another delivery mechanism, register <a href="#section-custom-mailer">custom sending logic</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p id="required-mailer-inputs">
Pass non-null values for custom sending logic, OAuth2 token providers, Email defaults/overrides and a supplied Session.
A null argument is rejected before replacing an earlier choice. Use <code>clearCustomMailer()</code>,
<code>clearOAuth2AccessTokenProvider()</code>, <code>useConfiguredEmailDefaults()</code> or <code>clearEmailOverrides()</code>
for removal or restoration; <code>withoutEmailDefaults()</code> selects an empty defaults template.
Nullable SMTP components follow their documented meanings.
</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-legacy-smtp-content" id="section-legacy-smtp-content" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Known tolerant legacy SMTP servers</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>Reuse one Mailer for ASCII and international mail; the bundled managed Angus transport negotiates each message separately.
        Only enable this compatibility option after verifying that the server and onward delivery route preserve internationalized addresses,
        raw UTF-8 headers and raw 8-bit bodies without advertising the corresponding extensions. It defaults to false:</p>
<pre><code class="small">mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withLegacySmtpContentSupport(true)
    .build());
boolean enabled = mailer.getOperationalConfig().isLegacySmtpContentSupportEnabled();</code></pre>
      <p>This does not relax TLS, REQUIRETLS, explicit DSN or content-safety checks. An accepted submission does not prove downstream delivery.
        In a shared cluster, the selected Session's configuration supplies this permission, not the Mailer you happened to call.
        Read the <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-international-mail">international-mail workflow and provider boundaries</a> before enabling it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#connection-conflicts" id="connection-conflicts" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Conflicting connection settings</h3>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>
<code>buildMailer()</code> rejects different explicit declarations of the same supported connection setting.
File and environment precedence is resolved before this check. Align the declarations, or choose
<code>ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code> when Java should replace an imported setting.
</p>
    <p>Read <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">how connection conflicts are handled</a> for the policy, partial customization and supplied Sessions.
      Locks and mandatory TLS requirements remain binding.</p>
  </div></section>

<a href="#section-proxy" id="section-proxy" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 socks=true}}Send using a proxy{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div>
      <p class="wide">
Configure a SOCKS5 proxy for SMTP, STARTTLS or SMTPS connections. Anonymous proxy support comes from
<a href="https://jakartaee.github.io/mail-api/FAQ.html#proxy">Jakarta Mail</a>;
proxy username/password authentication requires <code>authenticated-socks-module</code>.
</p>



    </div>

    <div>
<pre><code>// anonymous proxy
currentMailerBuilder.withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
    .withHost("proxy.host.com")
    .withPort(1080)
    .build());

// authenticated proxy
currentMailerBuilder.withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
    .withHost("proxy.host.com")
    .withPort(1080)
    .withUsername("proxy username")
    .withPassword("proxy password")
    .build());
</code></pre>
      <p>
Raw SOCKS host/port settings can combine with SJM proxy credentials when their endpoints agree.
Conflicting explicit endpoints are rejected by default. With
<code>ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>, typed Java members take precedence;
a Java host alone retains an applicable raw port. See the
<a href="/understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>.
</p>
      <p>With authentication, <code>mailer.getProxyConfig()</code> describes the remote proxy, while the effective Session connects to the local
        bridge. Its allocated loopback port is separate from the remote proxy port.</p>



      <p>See <a href="configuration.html#section-proxy-bridge-ports">Authenticated proxy bridge ports</a> for automatic loopback ports,
        optional fixed ports, and applications that use more than one authenticated-proxy Mailer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-proxy-bridge-ports" id="section-proxy-bridge-ports" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 socks=true}}Authenticated proxy bridge ports{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>If your worker reaches the mail relay through an authenticated SOCKS proxy, give the Mailer the remote host, port
        and credentials. By default, the operating system picks a free port for the local bridge on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, so different
        Mailers can run without coordinating local ports.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SocksProxyConfig proxy = SocksProxyConfig.builder()
    .withHost("proxy.example.com")
    .withPort(1080)
    .withUsername("proxy-user")
    .withPassword("proxy-password")
    .build();

Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "smtp-user", "smtp-password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withProxyConfig(proxy)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>When you need a fixed local port, pass the edited config to <code>withProxyConfig(...)</code> before building the Mailer.
        You can also set <code>simplejavamail.proxy.socks5bridge.port=1081</code> in your factory configuration.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SocksProxyConfig fixedPortProxy = proxy.toBuilder().withBridgePort(1081).build();</code></pre>
      <p><code>1080</code> is the remote proxy port in this example. The bridge port is a separate setting.
        Use <code>0</code> for automatic selection.</p>

      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Find the active port and troubleshoot proxy setup</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Inspect the port.</strong> Before opening SMTP, the Mailer writes the selected local port to
            <code>mail.smtp.socks.port</code> or <code>mail.smtps.socks.port</code> on its effective Session.
            An automatically selected port may change when the bridge restarts.</li>
          <li><strong>Check lengths and ranges.</strong> For active authenticated routes, the username and password each need
            1 through 255 UTF-8 bytes. Non-ASCII characters may take more than one byte. Active remote proxy ports must be
            1 through 65535 and local bridge ports 0 through 65535. <code>buildMailer()</code> checks the effective values before
            allocating owned resources. Building or inspecting a <code>SocksProxyConfig</code> only records or reads your choices.</li>
          <li><strong>Check proxy replies.</strong> Setup requires complete, valid authentication and CONNECT replies. A truncated
            CONNECT reply or invalid protocol field stops setup before SMTP begins. Proxy authentication traces omit the
            username/password packet regardless of Jakarta Mail's debug settings.</li>
          <li><strong>Check DNS and address handling.</strong> The local hop is IPv4 loopback. Remote proxies and destinations
            support IPv4 and IPv6. If local resolution fails, the provider/JDK can pass the domain name through SOCKS for remote
            resolution. Anonymous SOCKS routes use the provider directly. A typed anonymous IPv6 address stays intact on
            managed Angus connections. Supplied Sessions and custom factories follow their provider's handling.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Concurrent sends, timeouts and bridge shutdown</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Setup slots.</strong> Work through one Mailer shares its bridge. An established tunnel frees its setup slot.
            If setup is at capacity, the new setup is refused. Active tunnels remain usable, and idle setup workers can retire
            without extra configuration.</li>
          <li><strong>Timeouts.</strong> The SMTP client's socket timeouts govern its waits after SOCKS setup. The bridge adds
            no read or idle timeout. The pool's lifetime settings govern idle pooled connections.</li>
          <li><strong>Cleanup.</strong> A disconnect, timeout or cancellation during proxy setup closes that pending remote
            connection. Other connections are independent. With no active proxy operations, the listener stops accepting
            connections and closes unfinished setups from that listener. Established pooled or retained tunnels stay usable
            until their transports close.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-config-properties" id="section-config-properties" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Properties files</h2>

  <section>
      <p class="wide">Suppose a laptop and a production worker run the same sending code against different relays. Load the
        settings each one needs with <code>ConfigLoader</code>. Each factory has its own snapshot, so both can also have
        different message defaults and run in the same process.</p>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Use <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()</code> when you want the conventional setup. On its first call, it reads an
        optional <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.properties</code> resource from the classpath, then environment variables, then
        JVM system properties. It keeps that immutable snapshot for later calls and never consults Spring.
      </p>
      <p>To choose the sources yourself, add them to <code>ConfigLoader</code> from lowest to highest priority and call
        <code>load()</code>. A later non-blank value wins. Leaving the deployment username blank keeps the earlier username.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Earlier source
simplejavamail.smtp.username=relay-user

# Later source: does not clear relay-user
simplejavamail.smtp.username=</code></pre>
      <p>Null map values and whitespace-only strings are absent too. To deliberately remove a captured SMTP username in Java, use
        <code>clearSMTPServerUsername()</code>; the password remains a separate choice. Clearing both stored credentials requires
        <code>clearSMTPServerPassword()</code> as well. Removing an imported connection value requires the deliberate override policy;
        configuration locks still apply. See the
        <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-operations">operation table</a> for the distinction between clearing and restoring configuration.</p>
      <p>Simple Java Mail parses the winning value. If the file's port is malformed and the environment sets it to
        <code>587</code>, the loader uses the valid port.</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Lower priority: simplejavamail.properties
simplejavamail.smtp.port=not-a-number
                    ↓ overridden before parsing
Higher priority: environment variable
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
                    ↓ parsed
Loaded SMTP port: 587</code></pre>
      <p>
        The conventional short form is equivalent to:
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withClasspathResource("simplejavamail.properties")
    .withEnvironmentVariables()
    .withSystemProperties()
    .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);</code></pre>
      <p>
        Only add environment and system-property sources when you want them. Explicit loaders do not silently read either one.
        Mailer connection customization follows the <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>; Email defaults and overrides
        follow their separate <a href="#section-config-mailer">message rules</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
      Compose the sources your application owns:
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withClasspathResource("mail-base.properties")
    .withPropertiesFile(
        "deployment file",
        Path.of("config", "simplejavamail.properties"))
    .withProperties("runtime overrides", runtimeOverrides)
    .withMap("local overrides", localOverrides)
    .withSource(secretManagerSource)
    .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);</code></pre>
      <p>
<code>withPropertiesFile(Path)</code> reads the file on each <code>load()</code> and closes its stream.
<code>withInputStream(...)</code> consumes and closes the supplied stream immediately. Maps, <code>Properties</code> and custom sources
are sampled when <code>load()</code> runs; the returned snapshot keeps separate copies of their values.
</p>
      <p>A Java <code>Properties</code> input includes its inherited string defaults, including locked settings. Direct entries take precedence
        over those defaults, and the combined input is sampled again on each <code>load()</code>. The usual source ordering and validation still apply.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>If the application gets relay credentials from a secret manager, add a <code>ConfigSource</code> like the one below.
        Give it a stable name for diagnostics and return a map of canonical <code>simplejavamail.*</code> keys. Strict sources
        reject unknown keys. Environment and system-property sources allow unrelated entries.</p>
      <p>
        Secret lookup, decryption, and refresh policy belong in your application or configuration framework. Return the already decrypted values from
        the source. Simple Java Mail validates and copies them, but does not add a secret-encryption format of its own.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">ConfigSource secretManagerSource = new ConfigSource() {
    public String getName() {
        return "application secret manager";
    }

    public Map&lt;String, ?&gt; getProperties() {
        return secretManager.readMailSettings();
    }
};</code></pre>
      <p>
        Validation errors name the source and property key without printing the configured value. A snapshot's
        <code class="inline language-java">toString()</code> redacts passwords, key material, and arbitrary Session properties.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-config-snapshot" id="section-config-snapshot" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>When changes take effect</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>If you change the relay port in a file while invoices are in flight, those sends keep their settings. A
        <code>SimpleJavaMailConfig</code> is a detached, thread-safe snapshot. Changing a source or calling
        <code>load()</code> again leaves existing factories, builders, Emails, Mailers and Sessions on their captured
        settings.</p>
      <p>Load the updated properties, check the port, and build the replacement before routing new work to it.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig updated = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withProperties("invoice-worker", updatedProperties)
    .load();
System.out.println(updated.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT));
Mailer replacement = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(updated).mailerBuilder().buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>Have the application hand new jobs to <code>replacement</code>. Let callers already using the old Mailer finish, then
        <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-mailer-lifecycle">close it</a>. To change only part of a snapshot,
        add <code>withConfig(previous)</code> as the first source before loading your changes.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Inspect loaded settings, converters and S/MIME key reuse</summary>
<ul><li>Use <code>hasProperty(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT)</code> and <code>getPropertySource(...)</code> to see whether a source supplied the value. <code>asMap()</code> and its wildcard maps are read-only.</li><li><strong>Choose a converter.</strong> Static inbound methods on <code class="inline language-java">EmailConverter</code> use the conventional default snapshot. Use
          <code class="inline language-java">mail.converter()</code> when parsing should produce builders backed by an explicit factory snapshot.
          For example, <code>mail.converter().fromEml(emlPath).toEmailBuilder()</code> keeps that snapshot without restricting the supported conversion routes.
          See <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-converting">source-first conversion</a> for the complete shape and receive-side options.</li>
        <li><strong>Account for S/MIME keys.</strong> <a href="security.html#section-smime-key-cache">S/MIME key reuse</a> belongs to the factory. Its Mailers and converters share the
          selected <code>simplejavamail.smime.cache.capacity</code>; a separate factory has independent reuse even when it receives the same
          snapshot object. Change capacity by creating replacement configured objects. Reading
          <code>get(ConfigKeys.SMIME_KEY_CACHE_CAPACITY)</code> reports the captured input, so an absent value remains null while active use
          falls back to the published unlimited value <code>-1</code>.</li>
</ul>
</details>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
<h4 id="configuration-resolution-timing">When choices are combined</h4>
<p>Build a new factory and Mailer to use changed loaded settings. Ordinary <code>buildEmail()</code> creates the submitted Email; complete it explicitly or let the selected Mailer apply its templates during send preparation. <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-resolution-timing">Read the detailed explanation</a>.</p>
</div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-typed-config-keys" id="section-typed-config-keys" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Read configuration values in Java</h3>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Each <code>ConfigKeys</code> key carries its Java value type. Use the same key for reading, presence/source inspection
      and explicit/configured/fallback selection. The compiler rejects a port assigned to String or a String fallback for an Integer key.
      These are captured input values; built-in and derived Mailer defaults are resolved by the builders.
    </p>
<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withMap("deployment", Map.of(
        "simplejavamail.smtp.port", "587",
        "simplejavamail.defaults.sessiontimeoutmillis", "1500"))
    .load();

Integer port = config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT);
Duration timeout = config.get(ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT);
Integer selectedPort = config.valueOrProperty(null, ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT, 25);
String source = config.getPropertySource(ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT);
boolean configured = config.hasProperty(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT);
// String wrong = config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT); // does not compile</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
Time-valued keys return <code>Duration</code>. Numeric-millisecond inputs accept whole numbers within signed <code>Integer</code> bounds;
ISO-duration inputs follow their own precision and validation rules. Reading <code>1500</code> from a numeric key returns
<code>Duration.ofMillis(1500)</code>; the typed view does not change the input's units or parser.
Socket timeout keys are <code>ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT</code>, <code>CONNECT_TIMEOUT</code>, <code>READ_TIMEOUT</code> and
<code>WRITE_TIMEOUT</code>. A missing per-operation key returns null even if the shared key is present;
the Mailer resolves that fallback when creating its Session.
</p>
    <p class="wide">
      Missing scalars and aggregate configuration maps return null: an absent key does not fabricate an empty configured
      map or a runtime default. Present maps retain complete types—<code>Map&lt;String, String&gt;</code> for extra properties,
      <code>Map&lt;UUID, ConnectionPoolClusterConfig&gt;</code> for clusters, and <code>Map&lt;String, SimpleJavaMailConfig&gt;</code>
      for named Mailers—and are detached and unmodifiable. This configuration convention is separate from operation-result collections.
    </p>
<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Map&lt;String, String&gt; extras = config.valueOrProperty(
    null, ConfigKeys.EXTRA_PROPERTIES, Map.of()); // application fallback; the absent key stays absent

SimpleJavaMailConfig copied = ConfigLoader.builder().withConfig(config).load();
Duration sameTimeout = copied.get(ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT);</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      <code>valueOrProperty</code> keeps a nonempty explicit value, otherwise the configured value, otherwise its supplied
      fallback. An empty String is absent; whitespace Strings, zero and false retain their usual meaning. Explicit and
      fallback objects remain application-owned. Neither a read nor this helper changes sources or bypasses locks.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code>ConfigLoader.Property</code> and <code>config.asMap()</code> remain available for dynamic integration. That
      heterogeneous map retains parser values, including Integer milliseconds for the numeric timeout keys; ordinary Java
      reads use <code>get(ConfigKey&lt;T&gt;)</code>. Typed reads and raw maps can expose credentials: use
      <code>getDiagnostics()</code> for safe logging and source provenance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-environment-variables" id="section-environment-variables" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Environment variable names</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        For the fixed properties listed below, uppercase the property name and replace each dot with an underscore. There is no additional relaxed
        binding: the name comes directly from the documented property key.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two wildcard namespaces are different: <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.*</code> and
        <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.*</code> are scanned as literal names. An environment
        variable such as <code class="inline language-text">SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_EXTRAPROPERTIES_MAIL_SMTP_TIMEOUT</code> is therefore not recognized.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some process launchers can supply literal lowercase names containing dots, but support varies between shells, containers, and operating systems.
        For wildcard settings, prefer a property file, an exact dotted JVM <code class="inline language-text">-D</code> system property, or the Java API.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties">SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_TRANSPORTSTRATEGY=SMTP_TLS
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_DEFAULTS_FROM_ADDRESS=sender@example.com
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-available-properties" id="section-available-properties" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Available properties</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Most mailer settings have a property equivalent, so each environment can use different values without changing application code.
      </p>
	  <p>
		<code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls</code> only affects the optional STARTTLS upgrade used by plain
		<code class="inline language-text">SMTP</code>. It does not weaken <code class="inline language-text">SMTP_TLS</code>, OAuth2 transport, or
		<code class="inline language-text">SMTPS</code>. An explicit <code class="inline language-java">TlsConfig.Builder.withOpportunisticTLS(...)</code> choice
		must agree with other explicit declarations in strict mode; it replaces an ordinary conflicting value in override mode.
	  </p>
	  <p>
		For <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private_key_file_or_data</code>, prefix a path with
		<code class="inline language-properties">file:</code> or inline Base64-encoded key bytes with
		<code class="inline language-properties">base64:</code>. Without a prefix, the value is interpreted as a path or UTF-8 key data.
		The <a href="cli.html#selected-route-key-files">CLI daemon's reuse profile</a> also follows these spellings: replacing a selected
		<code>file:</code> key prevents reuse of a Mailer holding its previous bytes.
	  </p>
	  <p>
		The embedded-image flags describe their exact route: <code class="inline language-properties">outside.base.url</code> controls URL resolution and
		<code class="inline language-properties">outside.base.classpath</code> controls classpath resolution.
	  </p>
    </div>

