  <a href="#section-import-limits" id="section-import-limits" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Optional limits for received-mail imports</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Suppose the support inbox normally imports at most 1,000 parts. Set the default on the factory that owns its converter.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.conversion.import.maximumpartcount=1000</code></pre>

    <p>For an individual conversion, use <code>withImportLimitsConfig(...)</code> to change a limit. Omitted members inherit the captured
      factory value. Without a setting the limit is unlimited. To keep the cap fixed, put the same value in the locked namespace.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.locked.conversion.import.maximumpartcount=1000</code></pre>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Explicit cap of 1,000.</strong> The import can proceed because it agrees with the lock.</li>
      <li><strong>Cap of 500 or reset to unlimited.</strong> Both conflict with the fixed value before source consumption.
        Use the factory’s <code>getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> to see where the lock came from.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For a finite byte cap, pass raw EML or MSG. Part count and depth share one allowance across the selected nested messages.
      See <a href="/email-conversion.html#conversion-import-limits">how to apply a cap and check the result</a> and
      <a href="spring.html#section-spring-import-limits">Spring configuration</a>.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>CLI message files and which limits apply</summary>
<p><a href="cli.html#message-file-import-limits">Copying and replying to a file</a> use the request factory’s part and depth limits.
      A finite raw byte cap fails before reading file content on these MIME routes. Forwarding EML borrows MIME with ordinary import defaults inactive.
      Forwarding MSG uses its supported import limits. Factory import limits are independent of Email templates and sending limits.</p>
</details>

  </div></section>

	<a href="#section-config-mailer" id="section-config-mailer" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	<h2>Mailer level email defaults and overrides</h2>

  <section><div class="wide full-width-example">
    <p>The orders and accounts workers already send through the relay in <code>simplejavamail.smtp.host</code>. Most orders come from
      the orders mailbox, and an individual order can supply its own sender. An account statement must always use the accounts mailbox.</p>
    <p>Give the orders Mailer a <strong>default</strong> to fill a missing From. Give the accounts Mailer an <strong>override</strong>
      to replace the submitted From. Each template only needs the fields you want it to supply.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();
Email ordersDefault = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("orders@example.org").buildEmail();
Email accountsOverride = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("accounts@example.org").buildEmail();

try (Mailer orders = mail.mailerBuilder().withEmailDefaults(ordersDefault).buildMailer();
     Mailer accounts = mail.mailerBuilder().withEmailOverrides(accountsOverride).buildMailer()) {
    orders.sync().sendMail(orderConfirmation);
    accounts.sync().sendMail(statement);
}</code></pre>
    <p id="configuration-precedence">For a single field, the override wins, followed by the message’s own value, then the default.
      Recipients and resources can combine. A winning header supplies the entire value list for its name.
      See <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-precedence">how fields combine</a>.</p>
    <p>A supplied defaults template replaces the factory’s property-derived message defaults. To keep those values, start with
      <code>buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides()</code> and edit a copy. Ordinary <code>buildEmail()</code> leaves completion
      to the sending Mailer. In a <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-clustering">cluster</a>, configure the template on the Mailer that uses it.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Skip selected fields or both templates for one message</summary>
<p id="email-template-exclusions">If a one-off message should skip the Mailer's ordinary templates, set its
			  template options after you start the Email builder.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions.builder()
        .ignoringDefaults()
        .ignoringOverrides()
        .build())
    // add the email content
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
			<p>If a message should use a different From address while keeping other defaults, exclude just the fields you want
			  to control. This example excludes the default From and Reply-To and the overriding DKIM configuration.</p>
			<p>Use <code>EmailProperty.ALL_RECIPIENTS</code> to exclude ordinary To, Cc and Bcc additions together, or choose one recipient type
				to exclude only that type. Your Email's own recipients remain. Delivery-recipient overrides use the separate
				<code>OVERRIDE_RECEIVERS</code> selector, and none of these exclusions removes recipients required by locked properties.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">emailBuilder
	.withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions.builder()
	    .dontApplyDefaultValueFor(EmailProperty.FROM_RECIPIENT, EmailProperty.REPLYTO_RECIPIENT)
	    .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
	    .build())
</code></pre>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Address lists, disabling defaults, CLI key files and exact EML</summary>
<ul><li class="wide"><strong>Recipient names and lists.</strong> To/Cc/Bcc address defaults use the same complete-list interpretation as Java composition: quoted names, comma/semicolon separators
		    and RFC group members are supported in source order. A configured name applies to every parsed mailbox;
		    without it, embedded names are retained. From and bounce defaults remain single-mailbox choices. Parsing keeps malformed nonempty text
		    for the configured send-time validator; it does not check delivery or query DNS. See the
		    <a href="/email-composition.html#section-recipient-builders">recipient name and list examples</a>.</li>
		  <li class="wide"><strong>Omit or restore defaults.</strong> Use <code>withoutEmailDefaults()</code> to omit ordinary message defaults, or <code>useConfiguredEmailDefaults()</code>
		    to return to the factory's captured defaults. Configuration locks remain binding. See
		    <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-operations">editing template choices</a> for more specific changes.</li>
		  <li class="wide"><strong>Unused signing files in the CLI.</strong> The CLI excludes unused ordinary signing/encryption files from its Mailer reuse identity when you select
		    <code>--mailer:withoutEmailDefaults</code>. <code>--mailer:clearEmailDefaults</code> restores the configured defaults.
		    Active or locked material is required in either case; see <a href="/cli.html#disabled-default-key-files">CLI default-file selection</a>.</li>
		  <li class="wide"><strong>Relay a finished file.</strong> Sending, rehearsing or exporting the original <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">exact Email</a> bypasses ordinary message templates. Its supplied bytes are already final,
		    so Mailer defaults, overrides, configured content validation, and security transformations cannot change them. Put any required visible headers in the
		    EML before building the exact Email, and configure only its SMTP envelope on the exact builder.</li>
		  <li class="wide"><strong>Apply templates to a parsed view.</strong> Exact Email retains its separate byte-preservation contract. <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#repeated-email-completion">Read the completion and conversion details</a> before applying ordinary templates to its parsed view.</li>
		</ul>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Require a recipient-rejection policy</summary>
<p>For recipient-rejection handling, prefer the Email API over a raw provider property:
        <code>SmtpDeliveryOptions.Builder.withSendingToAcceptedRecipients(true)</code> continues for accepted recipients, and <code>false</code> withholds content after
        a known recipient rejection. Configure <code>simplejavamail.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients</code> for an ordinary Email default, or
        <code>simplejavamail.locked.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients</code> to keep the choice fixed within this factory. Ordinary defaults can be
        overridden or suppressed; a lock cannot. See <a href="analyzing-send-results.html#section-partial-send">both choices and their resulting receipts</a>,
        including why an advanced <code>sendpartial=true</code> property conflicts with an Email choosing false.</p>
</details>
    <p id="repeated-email-completion">A second completion applies the selected Mailer’s templates again. For a normal send, pass the original submitted Email.
      For preserved EML, keep the original exact Email. See <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#repeated-email-completion">what a second pass keeps or adds</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-combine-config" id="section-combine-config" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Combining everything for multiple environments</h2>

  <section>
    <div>
      <p>Say developers use a local mail catcher, acceptance tests use a staging relay, and production uses the company relay.
        Put the shared choices in one file and load the selected environment's settings last.</p>
      <h3>Properties for the environments</h3>
    </div>
    <div>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">#global default properties (simplejavamail.properties on classpath)

    # anonoymous SMTP inside 'safe' DMZ
    simplejavamail.smtp.host=dmz.smtp.candyshop.com
    simplejavamail.smtp.port=25

    # default sender and reply-to address
    simplejavamail.defaults.from.name=The Candy App
    simplejavamail.defaults.from.address=candyapp@candystore.com
    simplejavamail.defaults.replyto.name=Candystore Helpdesk
    simplejavamail.defaults.replyto.address=helpdesk@candystore.com
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">#overrides from TEST and UAT .../config/candystore/simplejavamail.properties

    # always send a copy to test archive
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.name=Archive TST UAT
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.address=test-archive@candyshop.com
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">#overrides from PRODUCTION .../config/candystore/simplejavamail.properties

    # always send a copy to production archive
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.name=Archive PRODUCTION
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.address=prod-archive@candyshop.com

    # smtp server in production is protected
    simplejavamail.smtp.username=creamcake
    simplejavamail.smtp.password=crusty_l0llyp0p

    # sending mails in production must go through proxy
    simplejavamail.proxy.host=proxy.candyshop.com
    simplejavamail.proxy.port=1080
    simplejavamail.proxy.username=candyman
    simplejavamail.proxy.password=I has the sugarcanes!!1!
</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Now for the programmatic part</h3>

    <div>
      <p>
        Build the complete source list for the selected environment, then load one snapshot and create the factory that owns it. The order below goes
        from broad defaults to the most deployment-specific overrides.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ConfigLoader loader = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withClasspathResource("simplejavamail.properties")
    .withPropertiesFile(
        "selected environment",
        Path.of("config", "candystore", "simplejavamail.properties"));

if (someSpecialCondition) {
    loader.withClasspathResource("special-override.properties");
}

Properties runtimeOverrides = new Properties();
runtimeOverrides.setProperty("simplejavamail.smtp.port", "2525");
SimpleJavaMailConfig config = loader
    .withProperties("runtime overrides", runtimeOverrides)
    .withEnvironmentVariables()
    .withSystemProperties()
    .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    // Deliberately replace the captured SMTP port for this Mailer.
    .withConnectionConflictPolicy(ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES)
    .withSMTPServerPort(587)
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .buildMailer();
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>


