  <a href="#section-locked-configuration" id="section-locked-configuration" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Keep centrally supplied settings fixed</h2>
  {{> components/pattern-examples topic="locked-configuration"}}
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>Suppose your application should always use the company relay and include an archive mailbox, while individual jobs choose their own
        subjects and recipients. Put the fixed values under <code>simplejavamail.locked.</code>, followed by the existing property's name.
        The value is the actual setting, not a true/false marker saying whether it is locked.</p>
      <p>Every Mailer created from the same factory inherits these restrictions. An ordinary property cannot replace its locked counterpart,
        even when the ordinary property comes from a higher-priority source. Competing locked entries still follow normal source precedence;
        a blank entry does not clear an earlier value. Supplying the same parsed value in Java is allowed; a conflicting customization fails.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Replacement is allowed with the deliberate override policy:
simplejavamail.smtp.port=587

# Fixed for this factory:
simplejavamail.locked.smtp.host=relay.company.com
simplejavamail.locked.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS
simplejavamail.locked.defaults.bcc.address=archive@company.com

# Optional, if the server supports onward REQUIRETLS:
# simplejavamail.locked.defaults.requiretls=true</code></pre>
      <p>Environment variables follow the existing naming convention, for example <code>SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_LOCKED_SMTP_HOST</code>.
        Spring supports the same values through <a href="spring.html#section-spring-locked-configuration">nested YAML or properties</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(
    ConfigLoader.builder().withPropertiesFile(Path.of("mail.properties")).load());

try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder().buildMailer()) {
    Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
        .from("orders@company.com")
        .withRecipients(mail.recipientBuilder().withAddress("customer@example.org").withType(Message.RecipientType.TO).build())
        .withSubject("Your order")
        .withPlainText("Thanks for your order.")
        .buildEmail();
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email); // Also includes archive@company.com.
}

// Fails when building the Mailer, before opening a connection:
mail.mailerBuilder().withSMTPServerHost("another-relay.example.org").buildMailer();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>The order keeps its visible To address. The lock adds the archive mailbox to the SMTP envelope.</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">Visible To: customer@example.org
SMTP envelope: customer@example.org
               archive@company.com</code></pre>

      <p>The archive copy stays required even if the job chooses a different delivery list. A Java choice for a different relay fails while
        building the Mailer, before it opens a connection.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Recipient locks, address names and conflict diagnostics</summary>
<ul>        <li><strong>Keep the archive recipient.</strong> The archive recipient is additive. Your other recipients remain, and an explicit delivery-recipient override still includes the archive.
          Repeated preparation does not keep adding another copy. Ignoring defaults or overrides, replacing a template, or completing an Email early
          does not remove locked settings.</li>
        <li id="interpreted-sender-locks"><strong>Set address and name separately.</strong> A locked From or bounce value can include a name, such as <code>Accounts &lt;accounts@company.com&gt;</code>.
          The lock fixes the parsed address. The name remains an ordinary default unless separately locked, so an equivalent named or bare address
          satisfies the address lock. Different mailboxes are rejected; normal address case rules and exact-message preservation apply.</li>
        <li><strong>Trace a rejected change.</strong> Conflicts name the locked property and its source without printing either value. The factory's
          <code>getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> shows winning locked entries with their original source and normal secret redaction.
          See <a href="debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">configuration provenance</a> for examples.</li>
</ul>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Failover, transport boundaries and telemetry</summary>
<p class="wide">A lock prevents accidental changes within this factory; it is not a security sandbox. Applications can still create an independent factory.
      <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#locked-requirements">Read the lock boundaries</a> for exact EML, supplied Sessions, custom transports and destination changes.</p>
    <p class="wide">For more than one approved SMTP destination, see <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-named">named failover configuration</a>.
      Each <code>simplejavamail.mailers.name.*</code> definition resolves independently. A locked relationship protects the captured route, while
      mandatory message locks remain binding through destination changes. Ordinary templates belong to the selected Mailer, including in a cluster.</p>
    <p class="wide">Optional <a href="/opentelemetry.html#section-privacy">OpenTelemetry endpoint permission</a> uses
      <code>simplejavamail.telemetry.smtp.endpointattributes</code> and its locked counterpart. Activate reporting for Mailers from this factory
      with <code>simplejavamail.telemetry.enabled=true</code>, using the application's initialized global instance or an explicit Java registration.
      Both Boolean settings support locks. The initiating Mailer resolves them independently of the selected SMTP destination.</p>
</details>

    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-custom-session" id="section-custom-session" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Sending with your own Session instance{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>If your app server already supplies a Jakarta Mail <code>Session</code>, hand it to <code>mailerBuilder(session)</code>.
        The Mailer keeps that Session, its Authenticator and its providers.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder(yourSession).buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Keep what the Session supplies.</strong> Timeouts, local binding, EHLO hostname and certificate trust
          come from the Session. Ordinary factory defaults leave those values and omissions in place. Deliberate
          customization and applicable locks can add settings.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose overrides deliberately.</strong> Conflicting declarations fail at construction by default.
          Select <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code> when you mean to replace a setting. Locks still apply.</li>
        <li><strong>Name the protocol for SJM options and probes.</strong> Set <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> to
          <code>smtp</code> or <code>smtps</code> when using SJM's protocol-specific options or the
          <a href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Reuse the Mailer for concurrent sends.</strong> One Mailer at a time owns the Session and its
          <code>Properties</code>. Wait for successful shutdown before using either with a new Mailer.</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Timeout overrides, custom protocols and event executors</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Override only what you need.</strong> With <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>,
            <code>withSessionTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1))</code> on a <code>ConnectionConfig.Builder</code> sets all
            three socket timeouts. <code>withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))</code> alone changes only reads.
            See the <a href="understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a> for
            raw properties and equivalent settings, and the
            <a href="configuration.html#section-programmatic-api-other">connection configuration guide</a> for timeout inspection.</li>
          <li><strong>Set or leave the protocol.</strong> Set <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> on the Session or
            with the Mailer builder's <code>withProperty(...)</code>. Conflicts and locks still apply. With it unset,
            ordinary sends can use the Session's selected transport and SJM reports protocol-specific settings as unspecified.
            Use provider-specific properties for a custom protocol.</li>
          <li><strong>Configure events before construction.</strong> When <code>mail.event.scope=session</code>, supply
            the event executor before creating the Session. The Mailer builder rejects a replacement. See
            <a href="configuration.html#session-construction-properties">using your own event executor</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Session ownership during construction and cleanup</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>Keep the Session stable during <code>buildMailer()</code>. Construction rejects a changed baseline.
            Configuration and shared sending-limit rules are checked before Session settings are applied. New allowance rules
            are kept only after the whole failover route builds successfully.</li>
          <li>If setup fails after settings are applied, the builder restores changed properties, OAuth callbacks and native
            debug settings. It retains ownership while cleanup and restoration are in progress, and a competing build is
            rejected. If cleanup fails, use a fresh Session with independent Properties.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-custom-properties" id="section-custom-properties" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Setting custom properties on the internal Session{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
	<section>
		<div class="view">
			<p>
				Then there are extra properties which will directly go on the internal Session object when
				building a Mailer instance.
			</p>
			<p>
				They use the same precedence as the other settings: system properties win over environment variables, and environment variables win over
				property-file values.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.my.extra.property=value
simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.ssl.socketFactory.class=org.mypackage.MySSLSocketFactory
simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.timeout=30000
</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>


	<section>
		<div class="view">
			<p>Use raw Jakarta Mail properties when a provider setting has no typed Mailer option. If you already have a
			  properties file with SMTP timeouts, these same methods let you carry the settings over.</p>
      <p>With otherwise default configuration, the example selects a 10-second connect timeout and a 30-second read timeout;
        the write timeout stays at 60 seconds. Raw timeout properties can replace built-in SJM defaults.
        For typed fine-tuning, use <code>ConnectionConfig.Builder.withConnectTimeout(...)</code>, <code>withReadTimeout(...)</code>
        and <code>withWriteTimeout(...)</code>. Different explicit declarations fail under the default <code>REJECT_CONFLICTS</code> policy.
        With <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>, an explicit typed choice wins over its raw counterpart;
        <code>withSessionTimeout(...)</code> explicitly selects all three. Applicable locks remain enforced in either mode.
        See <a href="configuration.html#socket-timeouts">socket timeout examples and inspection</a>.</p>
		</div>

		<div class="side">
<pre><code>currentMailerBuilder
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.timeout", 30 * 1000)
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.connectiontimeout", 10 * 1000)
    // or
    .withProperties(yourPropertiesObject)
    .withProperties(yourPropertiesMap)
</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="view">
			<p>To configure the same raw options in a property file, prefix each provider key with <code>simplejavamail.extraproperties.</code>.</p>
			<p>
				Properties prefixed with <em>simplejavamail.extraproperties.</em> supply additional Session settings. Recognized connection settings
        participate in the <a href="understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>; unknown provider extensions retain their provider-defined behavior.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.extraproperties.my.extra.property=value
simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.ssl.socketFactory.class=org.mypackage.MySSLSocketFactory
simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.timeout=30000
</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>

<a href="#section-local-bind-address" id="section-local-bind-address" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Binding the local/source SMTP address{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
	  <div class="view">
		  <p>
			  On machines with multiple local IP addresses, the outgoing SMTP socket can be bound to a specific local/source address.
			  Simple Java Mail applies this to the right Jakarta Mail property for the configured transport strategy.
		  </p>
		  <p>
			  Leave the local port empty unless you specifically need it. To specify both in Java, use <code>withLocalBindAddress("203.0.113.10", 25252)</code>. Binding a fixed local port can conflict with another connection.
		  </p>
	  </div>

	  <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
        .withLocalBindAddress("203.0.113.10")
        .build())
    .buildMailer();
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.localaddress=203.0.113.10
simplejavamail.smtp.localport=25252
</code></pre>
	  </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-smtp-client-hostname" id="section-smtp-client-hostname" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Setting the SMTP EHLO/HELO client hostname{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
  {{> components/pattern-examples topic="smtp-client-identity"}}

  <section>
	  <div class="view">
		  <p>
			  Corporate SMTP relays sometimes expect a stable, recognizable client hostname in the SMTP <code class="inline language-text">EHLO</code> or
			  <code class="inline language-text">HELO</code> command. Even when the relay does not enforce it, this makes application traffic recognizable
			  in mail-server logs, tracing, audit trails and SMTP diagnostics.
		  </p>
		  <p>
			  This is the SMTP protocol identity and maps to <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.localhost</code> or
			  <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtps.localhost</code> depending on the transport strategy. It is not the same as
			  <a href="#section-local-bind-address">binding the local/source socket address</a>, and it does not change the SMTP envelope sender.
		  </p>
	  </div>

	  <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
        .withSmtpClientHostname("orders-service.prod.example.com")
        .build())
    .buildMailer();
</code></pre>
<p>To remove the Java choice, use <code>clearSmtpClientHostname()</code> on the connection config builder.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.clienthostname=orders-service.prod.example.com
</code></pre>
	  </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-session-access" id="section-session-access" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Direct access to the internal Session{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        A built Mailer exposes the Jakarta Mail Session it actually uses. This is useful for integrations that need a Session API or property that is not
        represented directly by the Mailer builder. Prefer configuring it before the Mailer begins concurrent work: the same Session is shared by its sends.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code>Mailer mailer = ...;

Session session = mailer.getSession();
// do your thing with session
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-extension-points" id="section-extension-points" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Integration and extension points{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section class="extension-point-overview">
    <p class="wide">
      Stay with the high-level API for the parts it already handles, then take control at the boundary your application actually needs. These hooks range
      from replacing one policy around the Mailer to owning the final submission operation; using one does not require abandoning the rest of the pipeline.
    </p>

    <dl class="extension-boundary-map">
      <div>
        <dt><span>Mailer runtime</span><strong>Replace policy and execution services.</strong></dt>
        <dd>
          <p>Supply your own address validator, executor service, or terminal send observer while normal message preparation and submission remain in place.</p>
          <nav class="extension-boundary-links" aria-label="Mailer runtime extension documentation">
            <a href="/debugging.html#section-email-validation">Validation policy</a>
            <a href="sending-and-execution.html#section-batch-and-clustering">Executor service</a>
            <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-mail-send-observer">Send observer</a>
          </nav>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><span>Jakarta Session</span><strong>Bring your own or add what is missing.</strong></dt>
        <dd>
          <p>Supply a preconfigured Session, including its Authenticator and provider selection, or add raw Jakarta Mail properties to the managed Session. The built Mailer also exposes the Session for integrations that need the Jakarta object itself.</p>
          <nav class="extension-boundary-links" aria-label="Jakarta Session extension documentation">
            <a href="#section-custom-session">Supply a Session</a>
            <a href="#section-custom-properties">Add raw properties</a>
            <a href="#section-session-access">Access the managed Session</a>
          </nav>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><span>Message boundary</span><strong>Work directly with MIME and EML.</strong></dt>
        <dd>
          <p>Convert between Email, MimeMessage, and EML for inspection, storage, interoperability, or further processing without giving up the higher-level message model.</p>
          <nav class="extension-boundary-links" aria-label="MIME and EML extension documentation">
            <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-converting">Convert message formats</a>
            <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">Submit finalized EML</a>
          </nav>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><span>Connection setup</span><strong>Control sockets and live credentials.</strong></dt>
        <dd>
          <p>Provide an SSLSocketFactory for a managed Session or a thread-safe OAuth2 token provider that supplies current credentials whenever a physical connection opens.</p>
          <nav class="extension-boundary-links" aria-label="Connection extension documentation">
            <a href="security.html#section-custom-ssl-factory">SSL socket factory</a>
            <a href="/security.html#section-authentication-methods">OAuth2 token provider</a>
          </nav>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><span>Submission boundary</span><strong>Replace the provider bridge or the whole send operation.</strong></dt>
        <dd>
          <p>A MailTransportAdapter keeps Simple Java Mail's transport lifecycle while adapting final submission to another Jakarta Mail provider. A CustomMailer instead owns connection testing and sending itself.</p>
          <nav class="extension-boundary-links" aria-label="Submission extension documentation">
            <a href="modules.html#mail-transport-adapter-spi">Transport adapter SPI</a>
            <a href="#section-custom-mailer">Custom sending logic</a>
          </nav>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

<a href="#section-custom-mailer" id="section-custom-mailer" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Plug your own sending logic{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
  {{> components/pattern-examples topic="custom-mailer"}}

	<section>
		<p class="wide">Suppose your service hands mail to an HTTP gateway. Plug a <code>CustomMailer</code> into the Mailer
		  to test that connection and submit the message. Simple Java Mail still uses its configured Session to prepare the
		  <code>MimeMessage</code>, with validation, signing and encryption. Synchronous and asynchronous sends call the
		  callback.</p>
		<ul>
		  <li class="wide"><strong>Manage the gateway connection.</strong> Call <code class="inline language-java">mailer.sync().sendMail(...)</code> as usual. The CustomMailer owns the sending connection.
		    <code class="inline language-java">withOpenConnection(...)</code> is unavailable in this setup; keep any connection reuse inside the CustomMailer
		    implementation.
		    Simple Java Mail leaves retained SMTP OAuth credentials unused; your implementation owns authentication. Installing the batch module does not change this.</li>
		  <li class="wide"><strong>Read the receipt as prepared intent.</strong> The submission receipt retains the recipient intent prepared before this callback, with unknown acceptance and no SMTP replies.
		    Your implementation may choose a different external delivery list. Changing the supplied MIME message does not turn that prepared
		    intent into evidence of what your service accepted.</li>
		  <li class="wide"><strong>Keep finalized bytes.</strong> For exact EML, the callback receives the canonical <code>Email</code> and a <code>MimeMessage</code> backed by the authoritative input bytes.
		    Your implementation must preserve those bytes when handing the message to its service.</li>
		</ul>

        <div class="wide">
          <p>This <code>CustomMailer</code> shows where the Mailgun calls go. Replace the two placeholder methods with your API calls before running the send.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailgunMailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
        .withCustomMailer(new MailgunMailer())
        .buildMailer()) {
    mailgunMailer.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">public class MailgunMailer implements CustomMailer {
    @Override
    public void testConnection(OperationalConfig config, Session session) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Call your Mailgun connection check here");
    }

    @Override
    public void sendMessage(OperationalConfig config, Session session, Email email, MimeMessage message) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Submit the prepared message with your Mailgun client here");
    }
}</code></pre>
      </div>
	</section>

